Installation (Debian or Ubuntu with systemd, as root)

Use a clean server and clean VPS hosts that run nothing else: tun reconfigures them completely. On the server
it resets iptables on every start, takes over the routing tables 50, 51, 100, 101 and up (one per channel), 200
and 300, the rule priorities 50, 51, 90, 91, 100, 101 and 200 and the nexthop ids 3000-3999, turns on IP forwarding (kept off at boot by /etc/sysctl.d/99-tunnelcontrol.conf, on only after its firewall) and turns off IPv6; on every VPS it changes sshd_config, adds its key for root, turns on IP forwarding, turns off IPv6 and resets
iptables (see VPS below). After installation the network is configured so that the traffic of the local network
never goes out directly again, only through the tunnels: while they are stopped, the clients have no internet,
not even ping. The VPS hosts cannot open connections into the local network.

curl -fsSL https://tunnelcontrol.app/install.sh | bash
                    # installs the latest release from GitHub into /opt/tunnelcontrol and creates tun.config
                    # from the example; another folder: ... | TUNNELCONTROL_DIR=/path bash
                    # then fill in CONFIG and TUNNELS in tun.config and run tun --install
git clone https://github.com/rdpex-com/tunnelcontrol.git /opt/tunnelcontrol && /opt/tunnelcontrol/tun --install
                    # the same as a copy of the repository; the first tun --install creates tun.config and the tun command, then stops
WEB_PANEL=0         # in tun.config before tun --install: console only, without the web panel and Node.js


Commands

tun --install       # installation and initialization: dependencies (with the web panel also Node.js 20+ and its packages,
                    # installed again only when package-lock.json or the Node.js version changed),
                    # services, first VPS setup (asks for the VPS password); without tun.config it creates one from
                    # the example and stops. Running it again is safe: the config and data are not touched
tun --start         # start the tunnels
tun --restart       # restart the tunnels
tun --stop          # graceful stop of the tunnels
tun --kill          # immediate stop of the tunnels
tun --web-password  # set the web panel login and password (signs out every panel session)
tun --web-cert      # new panel certificate authority and site certificate; install the new certificate on
                    # every device again (Download Certificate in the panel)
tun --update        # update to the latest release: compares VERSION with UPDATE_URL and downloads the archive when
                    # the latest release differs from the installed version (a copy of the repository takes its latest code instead), then runs tun --install
                    # without questions (no e-mail, no root password: a VPS that needs its first setup is held off
                    # until tun --install is run by hand); tunnels that were stopped before the update stay stopped;
                    # only the code is replaced, tun.config, tun.server.setup.<VPS IP>, data and bak/ are not touched;
                    # an archive install keeps the list of release files in .release-files, and an update
                    # removes the files that left the release (a copy of the repository updates its files itself);
                    # once a day the server asks UPDATE_URL for the latest release (UPDATE_CHECK=1, not for
                    # copies of the repository) and tells about a newer one: a panel banner (Hide closes it for that
                    # version), Update on the System tab and in tun --status, one push notification
tun --version       # installed version
tun --help          # this list of commands; tun without a command or with an unknown one prints it too
tun --status        # whether the tunnels run from the console or as the tun service, the service, the web panel,
                    # then Global Stats, Active Tunnels and System Information as in the web panel: every host
                    # with its tunnels (state, port, weight, speed, traffic), CPU, RAM, disk and host data
tun --monitor [N]   # the same, redrawn every N seconds (3 by default); keys: 1-9 turn the host with that number
                    # on or off (as tun --toggle), q quits
tun --toggle <IP>   # turn a host on or off, like its switch in Active Tunnels of the web panel: the tunnels of a
                    # turned off host leave the routing and keep rotating as usual; a host cannot be turned off
                    # while it is the only one with working tunnels or the last enabled one, a host whose
                    # tunnels are all down can; holds until the tunnels restart; without an address lists the
                    # hosts with their state


Settings

tun.config          # your config, created from tun.config.example; never overwritten by updates;
                    # tun does not start while it is identical to the example
tun.defaults        # default values of all settings with descriptions; do not edit it —
                    # to change a setting, add a line with it to tun.config, for example ROTATION_DELAY=30
tun.config.check    # config check; tun does not start if the config has anything but settings or a value that
                    # does not fit its setting (address, port, range, interface name, number, unit), a
                    # setting given twice, or no CONFIG or no channel in TUNNELS; it names
                    # the line and what is wrong; the panel runs the same check before saving
                    # and while you type, highlighting the rejected lines
Values in tun.config may be in double quotes, in single quotes or without quotes ("eth0:192.168.0.0/16",
'eth0:192.168.0.0/16' and eth0:192.168.0.0/16 are the same); a value with a space, ; or & needs quotes.

Channel N uses the addresses 10.N.N.1 (server) and 10.N.N.2 (VPS), so a local network in 10.0.0.0/8 must not
overlap them; 192.168.0.0/16 and 172.16.0.0/12 are safe. This is not checked.
TUNNELS in tun.config has one line per channel. Three lines per VPS are recommended for the smoothest
rotation: it reconnects one channel at a time, and the other channels of that VPS carry its traffic meanwhile.
TUNNEL_ROUTING      # how the client route through the tunnels is kept: group = a resilient nexthop group, where
                    # the kernel drops a fallen channel at once and leaves the connections of the other channels
                    # where they are; route = one multipath route, rebuilt when a channel falls; auto (default) =
                    # group when the kernel supports it (checked at every start), otherwise route
TUNNEL_KILL_MODE    # how a live channel is closed on rotation, stop or a lost tunN: local (default) = ssh -O exit
                    # from this server; remote = first kill -9 the sshd of the channel on the VPS through the
                    # session, then ssh -O exit
DATA_PATH in tun.config is the data folder; when the path changes, the data moves on the next start.
Web panel settings are WEB_* in tun.defaults: login, address and ports, HTTPS, push notifications,
certificate address and validity, the folder for the copies of tun.config made before each save
(WEB_CONFIG_BACKUP_PATH: bak by default, /run/tunnelcontrol/bak keeps them in memory, empty keeps none).
A change in tun.config applies when the tunnels start or restart (tun --restart or Restart in the panel).
When a WEB_* setting or LOG_LEVEL differs from what the panel started with, the panel restarts with them;
the site certificate of the panel is issued again when its address (WEB_CERT_ADDRESS, or the IP of the CONFIG
interface) or WEB_CERT_SITE_DAYS changed (a warning in the log; the devices keep trusting it). WEB_CERT_DAYS, the validity of the authority installed on the
devices, applies on tun --web-cert; WEB_PANEL on tun --install.


Logs

LOG_LEVEL=1         # in tun.config: 0 = errors and warnings; 1 (default) = plus key events: install, start and
                    # stop, VPS setup, update; 2 = plus all other messages; 3 = plus debug messages
/var/log/tun.log    # errors and warnings at every level, key events from LOG_LEVEL=1
/var/log/tun.verbose.log   # every message, from LOG_LEVEL=2
/var/log/tun.mon.total.log # total traffic every 10 seconds, from LOG_LEVEL=2; the events of the traffic
                           # monitor are in tun.log and tun.verbose.log
/var/log/tun.mon.verbose.log, tun.mon.debug.log   # traffic accounting details, from LOG_LEVEL=3
/var/log/tun.web.log       # web panel log; its request log from LOG_LEVEL=3
/etc/logrotate.d/tun       # added by tun --install: a log over 10 MB is rotated at the daily logrotate run,
                           # two compressed copies are kept
LOG_VPS_FILE        # log file on every VPS, /var/log/tun.log by default; LOG_VPS_FILE="/tmp/tun.log" leaves
                    # nothing behind after a VPS reboot
LOG_VPS_MAX_KB      # each setup of a VPS (start, restart, recovery) trims its log when it is larger than this
                    # many KB, keeping the last half; 1024 by default, 0 = never trim
The Logs tab of the web panel lists the main log and the other log files that exist, except the panel's own
tun.web.log (at level 3 reading it would keep adding to it). After the level is lowered, it also warns how much
the logs of the higher level take in /var/log, until they are deleted.


VPS

The VPS must be clean: tun manages its firewall. On every VPS setup (tunnel start, setup of a VPS
that went down) iptables is reset and incoming connections are closed except ping, SSH on ports 22
and PORT, and the PORT_RANGE range for the local server (PORT and PORT_RANGE come from TUNNELS).
tun keeps its VPS script and marks in /run/tunnelcontrol on the VPS: in memory, gone after a VPS reboot,
and set up again with the VPS.

A VPS serves one local server at a time. The server keeps a random session mark until it reboots
(/run/tunnelcontrol/session, copied to data/tun_session), and the first connection of a VPS setup records it in
/run/tunnelcontrol/session on the VPS. Another server, or a copy of this one, that tries to set up the VPS while its
tunnels are up or its setup job runs is refused and touches nothing there; a setup job this server started before
its own reboot (same key, new mark) is taken over and waited for instead. The VPS becomes free when the tunnels and
the setup job of its server are gone or the VPS reboots.
The refusal names the first eight characters of the mark of the server that holds the VPS; every server logs
its own as "Session mark: ..." at the tunnel start, so the logs show which server it is. The push notification
leaves the mark out. A VPS that answers busy is asked once more after a minute before it is held off: after a
crash of this server (power loss, hard reset) its own old sessions stay on the VPS for up to 30 seconds under the
mark of the previous boot. Every VPS is set up on its own, so this wait, an unreachable VPS or a package install on
one VPS does not hold back the tunnels of the others; tun --start returns once every VPS has been tried (Ctrl-C
returns at once and leaves the setups running in the background). A setup
that takes longer than MANAGER_SETUP_TIMEOUT (900 seconds) is stopped and retried like any other failure.

Every VPS setup runs one job on the VPS, /run/tunnelcontrol/setup.job, apart from the ssh session: it installs the key
of this server, the sshd settings (keepalive, PermitTunnel; in /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00-tunnelcontrol.conf when
sshd_config includes that folder, otherwise in sshd_config, and checked with sshd -T; sshd is restarted only when
the check passes, and again when its configuration files changed after it started) and the missing packages and
kernel modules (apt: iproute2, iptables, procps, kmod, linux-modules-extra for the running kernel), showing the
progress. Each step is written to /run/tunnelcontrol/setup.log. When the link breaks, the job goes on; the next
attempt waits for it; a job that has ended runs again, so a VPS fixed after a failed step is set up again
on the next tunnel restart. When everything is in place the job takes seconds, so it runs at every setup. The setup
connects with the key of this server; a VPS that refuses the key needs the root password, which only
tun --install asks for (data/tun_host_<VPS IP>_configured marks a VPS that has taken the key).

A VPS used by another server, one that needs its first setup when no password was entered, one that rejects the
entered root password or does not let root log in with a password at all (PermitRootLogin prohibit-password,
PasswordAuthentication no; allow it once or put the key of this server there by hand), or one that lacks what
tunnels need, is held off: its
mark data/tun_host_<VPS IP>_held holds the reason (busy, setup, password or vps) on the first line and the message on the
second, the same message goes to the log and as one push notification, and the VPS is left out until
the tunnels are restarted (tun --start, --restart, --install): no retries, no rotation counting it, and its
tunnels are not listed in tun --status or in the panel.
After the setup job the VPS checks itself. Without /dev/net/tun, a tun interface, PermitTunnel, ip_forward, the NAT or
REDIRECT rule, the IN_IFACE interface (and OUT_IFACE with a PUBLIC_IP), or the ip, iptables or sysctl command, tunnels cannot work there: the VPS is held off with the
reasons in the message. Lesser problems (no conntrack or multiport rule, no pgrep or systemd-run) are only logged.
The root password entered in tun --install stays in memory only while a VPS still waits for its first
setup; once every VPS is set up or held off, it is cleared.

tun.server.setup.<VPS IP>   # optional file with your own commands for this VPS: open a port, an allow list,
                            # start your own scripts; runs as root on the VPS after the base firewall rules
                            # on every VPS setup. Unix line endings (LF) only. Updates do not touch it.
                            # Example line:
                            # iptables -I INPUT 1 -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT


Web panel

https://<server address>:8443
WEB_AUTH=1          # login with user and password (default); WEB_AUTH=0 in tun.config = no login
WEB_PUSH=0          # in tun.config: no push notifications; the panel does not create or load its keys, offers no
                    # button and drops the queued messages, tun --install does not ask for the e-mail
WEB_APP_NAME        # the panel installed as an app is named "Tunnel Control <WEB_APP_NAME>"; tun --install
                    # writes the host name of the server here, empty = the host name; up to 48 characters,
                    # without / : * ? < > |; servers need
                    # different names, or Chrome on Windows shows the notifications of the second app as
                    # its own
WEB_PUSH_LOGOUT_UNSUBSCRIBE=1   # in tun.config: Log Out in the panel also unsubscribes that device from push;
                    # by default a device keeps getting the notifications after Log Out
WEB_PUSH_EMAIL      # e-mail for push notifications; tun --install asks for it while it is the default
                    # root@localhost; Apple devices (iPhone, iPad, Safari on Mac) need a real address;
                    # after a change the devices stay subscribed
Enable Push Notifications   # button on the System tab in every browser that supports push, until this
                            # browser is subscribed (on iPhone and iPad only in the panel added to the home screen);
                            # Android and Chrome on a computer are asked for permission when the panel opens,
                            # Safari, Firefox and Edge after the click; when the browser blocks notifications for
                            # the panel, the button stays and its click says how to allow them
RUNNING / STOPPED           # panel header: the tunnel manager runs (data/tun_pid) as the tun service or from the
                            # console, also with its hosts held off or in setup; when it has not been running for
                            # 30 seconds, one push notification (a stop sends it; a restart
                            # by tun --restart, --install, --update or the panel buttons and the start of the panel
                            # send none)
https://<server address>:8443/cert.pem   # certificate of the panel's own certificate authority, to install on a
                                         # phone or a PC; it issues the site certificate of the panel, which
                                         # the panel renews by itself before it ends, also while the tunnels
                                         # run (WEB_CERT_SITE_DAYS), without a new install on the
                                         # devices. A panel installed before has one self-signed certificate
                                         # that Firefox rejects: tun --web-cert replaces it. On Windows into
                                         # Trusted Root Certification Authorities of the Local Machine, the
                                         # only store Firefox reads; the panel shows the steps per device
?debug=1 / ?debug=0 in the panel address # turn debug mode on / off (remembered in the browser; Hide in the
                                         # debug log turns it off)


